                               APPENDIX A
                        CHUCK MOORE'S ADDRESS

[The following is an edited excerpt of the principal points of Chuck
Moore's address to the committee:]

I am here to regale you.  I apologize for walking out a few meetings ago.
I got calls from some of you, but I have concluded that I am incompatible
with the standard's process.  FST, which I was a member of, and I had the
same reaction.  It seemed to be working this time.  The first meetings were
pleasant, because there was nothing of substance.  But the principle I
cannot subscribe to is that matters of fact or truth or beauty can be voted
on.  The facts speak for themselves.  That's my gut reaction.  I'm vitally
concerned you produce a good standard, but I cannot vote.

For example, MOVE .  MOVE is a simple concept, which can be made complex,
but needn't be.  MOVE must be a part of the standard, at least for
historical continuity.  But we originally designed MOVE on the NOVIX to
slurp 256 things and spit them out, then deal with the remainders.  I hated
it.  The supporting code took too much space, and was never used.  I
replaced it with a FOR ... NEXT move, which took 8 times as long in 1/4 of
the code, but it wasn't used anyway.  One of my criticisms is that, as a
committee, you tend to complexify things.

I have the most to gain from a successful standard, even though I cannot
participate in it, except in review or critique.  I think the process is
cumbersome, and I'm offended, even if its CBEMA's way.  But the ends
justify the means.  I suggest at each meeting, you have someone spend an
hour reviewing, like me, who is not involved in the committee.  Find some
objective outsiders.  I think you are too introverted.  Too enmeshed in the
process.  The problem with MOVE is hairsplitting.  It must not dominate the
process of the standard.

I suggest Flesch's book.  [The book was passed around.]  The BASIS can be
improved.  I have marked up a copy of BASIS6.  It is a beautiful document.
I appreciate how much work went into it, and that you are agonizing over
phrasing and words.

Consider page 1, "Purpose."  I feel this has not been adequately expressed.
I think an ANSI standard is relevant now, so that you can have a program
written on one personal computer and run it on another, or mix them with
mainframe programs.  Eliminate references to word size.  This isn't
mentioned in the purpose.  There should be a section indicating to whom
this document is addressed, implementors, users...  Who should spend the
money to buy the standard?  Focus on the audience.  For BASIS6, this is us.
It is not self-explanatory.  Do you want to reach a wide audience?

Page 1: preface "Forth" with "ANS Forth."  It is impossible for one version
of Forth to subsume all possible opportunities.  Forth is larger than the
standard.  I think ANS Forth will become the dominant dialect.  Computer
Cowboys will not follow it.  Shboom will likely be F-PC.  cmFORTH is likely
to be peculiar.  In coding recently, it is clear to me I'm writing Forth,
but it does not resemble historical Forth.  Don't take away the word Forth.

Page 2: I love the rationales, the double-boxes.  Single boxes might even
include comments from outsiders.  I suggest you publish the naked glossary
as part of the standard, and publish the unexpurgated version in an
appendix.  CBEMA would prefer a bulky document.

Regarding substitution of ISO for ASCII, I think it's a bad idea.  It
strikes me as jargon.  How about a footnote which says "when we say ASCII
we really mean ISO ..."  It's not good for PR.

 X3J14/89-023, Draft Minutes, X3J14 Meeting #7, January 23-28, 1989, Page 9


The Concepts section sort of went away.  What is Forth?  To pretend that
there is a necessary and sufficient definition of Forth is not helpful to
someone interested in Forth, who needs a concept section.

"Definition of terms, branch specification."  Defining is not helpful.  The
concept is not classic, nor is it in all systems.  You have to presume a
level of understanding of your audience.

"Byte":  it might not be a good move to use this word in an unusual sense.

"Cell pair," top of stack is at the lower address.  Can you standardize
this?  Does it matter?  Is requiring this going too far?  Doesn't it depend
on hardware?

"Compilation vocabulary."  Do you mean compiler vocabulary?  The more
syllables, the more you should question the word.

"Input stream."  I think this is obsolete.  Memories are getting large--you
don't need to fragment source into blocks.  Do you really need to specify
it?  A standard program shouldn't muck around with it.

[Mr. Braithwaite: is this a compiler writer's guide or technical user's
guide?]
You want a standard program to run on all standard systems.

Consistency?  FROT .  This is obscure, with low probability of use.  No
historical record.  FOR...NEXT is in the category of words you can't use.
These categories are arbitrary and require better reasons for inclusion or
exclusion.

Defining word: "when executed."  "External format."

"statically determinable"  These words should be replaced with a
circumlocution.

Division: "inexact".  Do you mean "commensurate"?

"Execution token"  Is this the result of great debates?  I'm not familiar
with it.

"Floating-point stack"  Shboom will do this on the data stack.  The standard
shouldn't fiat this.

[Mr. Bailey:  separate stacks can tackle this problem asynchronously.]

Exactly, but what are you being transportable amongst?  Just how many
different machines can you transport to?  An added stack is an expensive
thing.  You need the whole suite of words:  FDUP  FDROP  etc.  Consequences
have a habit of multiplying.

[Much discussion of the separate floating point stack.  Mr. Baden pointed
out that it is currently limited to four numbers.]

Page 9: "layer."  This is not a useful distinction.  Look at floating point,
for example.  Nothing is in the nucleus about this.

"Parameter field."  What this is is elusive to me when I try to be precise
about it.

[Mr. Dickens: everybody knows what a parameter field is, although it's tough
to get this knowledge into a paragraph.]

"Programming language processor"  Something written in Forth is not a
program in the sense it is in C.  It is a vocabulary.  The words that might
describe it have been preempted to describe something else.  I would like
to make this distinction.  I would like to contrast Forth with C.  Someone
ought to show that there are substantial differences.  In _Gravitation_, by
Kip Thorne, there are three ways of viewing


 X3J14/89-023, Draft Minutes, X3J14 Meeting #7, January 23-28, 1989, Page 10



gravity: tensor, vector, perception.  Forth and C are tensor and vector.
Forth uses MOVE where C would use subscripts.  C expands upon all the words
we tend to vary.  You see more conditionals and loops.

"Rounding and truncation" doesn't tell me anything.

Page 12: "finite time."  Numbers are accepted "unsigned or negatively
signed."  You should say they can't be positively signed.

"Implementation options," page 14, needs to be expanded on as a word.

"Operator's terminal facilities" has too many syllables.

"Minimum maximum" is marvelous.

Page 15: too many uses of "standard" in the same sentence with different
meanings.

Page 16: ...CREATE is redundant.

You are not allowed to DUMP since it accesses memory you haven't created.

[Ms Rather: DUMP is in the Reserved Word Set.]

One of Forth's virtues is that it can access any memory.

[Ms Rather: What about memory traps?]
The memory-mapped PDP-11 is old-fashioned hardware.

[Mr. Colburn: a human can look anywhere, but not a portable program.]
Say that explicitly, then.

Numbers, page 18, with "all bits set."  I don't find this helpful.

"Control structures cannot overlap"  Why not?  Mine do, by using  [ OVER ]
etc.

Error conditions: ignore, execute a particular word.  All of the others also
mean to execute a particular word.

"Compliance and Labeling," page 22.  Standard Program runs in a Standard
System, and all these variations of it are pedantic.  This is too
repetitious.

"Compactness" is an ugly word.

The presumption is that all Forth words are in upper case.

"Stack parameters."  My attitude is that you should leave them out
altogether.  They are so elaborate, they obscure what they mean in the first
place.  Given that you reject that, at least be consistent.  "False" is
spelled out, but not "addr" and "char".

Particularly be careful what you do when you describe the input stream and
the return stack.

6.8.2.2 <name> p. 24, why not just "name"?

Glossary.  Each word has been given two names.  For example, "number" can be
7.0020, "...see also 7.0020 number."  I would not look them up by number,
but rather by ASCII order.  Particularly in the glossary.

"Number-sign" should be called "number."

"Slash" not "divide."  Be sure you get the common pronunciation.

 X3J14/89-023, Draft Minutes, X3J14 Meeting #7, January 23-28, 1989, Page 11



Look at !  "Store w at addr" is not sufficient.

.(  "Parse and display characters" is a trivial use of a complex word.

Is 0<> really in wide use?

I object to 2ROT .

Why is EXIT called that instead of RETURN ?

7.0590 page 42 "set interpret state"  Interpreting is a large concept, as is
] .  They should be discussed somewhere.

Page 48  CLOSE-FILE .  Why bother?

"A Standard Program cannot use CODE ."  There aren't any Standard Programs.

CONVERT .  I haven't seen one like this before.  My stack effect is
different.

DNEGATE  "radix-complement of d1."  How about "0 minus d1"?
[Mr. Dickens: this language was taken from the IEEE dictionary.]

END-CODE  You can't use it, but you're trying to define it.

EVALUATE  I like it better than EVAL .

You did call F/ "f-slash".

Page 61, in  IF  " ...sys is balanced by the corresponding  ELSE  or  THEN
."  This is much too cryptic.

Why did INTERPRET go away?

OCTAL is in the Controlled Reference Wordset, but HEX is not.

[Both are "controlled".]

QUERY has a precise statement.  This is not appropriate.

RECURSE .  I would be willing to suggest you reverse the convention so that a
word is recursive unless otherwise indicated.

RENAME-FILE  why not use the OS to do this?

[Mr. Bailey: why should an application program have to hire a person to sit
down and go through DOS to do this?]

BLOCK extension wordset.  COPY is prevalent, but doesn't occur in the
standard.

SPAN and STATE I don't like.  I want them to go away.

WRITE-CR .  I'm appalled.  I don't understand files.

I'm done.

[Mr. Bailey:  Chuck, would you consider rejoining us when the BASIS is ready
for final wording?]

I will quibble on the spelling of many words.  The form is at least as
important.


 X3J14/89-023, Draft Minutes, X3J14 Meeting #7, January 23-28, 1989, Page 12



[Mr. Colburn:  Chuck, what do you see as the purpose of the standard?
Portability or political acceptance?]

I think we're here because we think a standard would be good for Forth.
I've heard C programs are not especially portable.

[Much discussion on the portability of C programs.  Mr. Rash had good
results and Mr. Forsley had bad results.]

The standard as a documentation and communication language is a laudable
goal...a useful tool that we've never had.

[Ms Rather: But you've run into massive FORTRAN programs that have been
around for 20 years, and everyone runs them on everything.  That's a
testimony to the fact that portability does occur, and people often complain
that none of these kinds of programs exist in Forth.  Hence Mr. Tracy's work
on the Model Library.]

You could describe a class of programs that don't transport.  C is mostly
preoccupied with ASCII files of text.  All the examples are this way.  We
don't do this in Forth.  I do mostly arithmetic, etc.  Porting into C would
take a major effort for me.



